  "reconstruction_text": "For every finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space E equipped with its compatible norm and Borel measurable structure, including dimension zero, let gamma be the canonical centered Gaussian measure with variance one in each orthonormal coordinate. For every signed real-valued function f on E which is globally twice continuously Frechet differentiable, assume f and its literal global gradient are genuinely in L2(gamma), and assume x -> f(x)^2 log(f(x)^2) is integrable with respect to gamma. Then\nintegral f(x)^2 log(f(x)^2) dgamma - (integral f(x)^2 dgamma) log(integral f(x)^2 dgamma) <= 2 integral ||gradient f(x)||^2 dgamma.\nThe gradient is the Riesz representation of the real Frechet derivative and the norm is the Hilbert norm. All integrals are real Bochner integrals; log is totalized at zero and the product t log t is interpreted continuously at t=0. The assertion requires no positivity of f or of its square mass, and no normalization, compact support, additional cutoff limit, or inequality certificate.",
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space E equipped with its compatible norm and Borel measurable structure, including dimension zero, let gamma be the canonical centered Gaussian measure with variance one in each orthonormal coordinate. For every signed real-valued function f on E which is globally twice continuously Frechet differentiable, assume f and its literal global gradient are genuinely in L2(gamma), and assume x -> f(x)^2 log(f(x)^2) is integrable with respect to gamma. Then\nintegral f(x)^2 log(f(x)^2) dgamma - (integral f(x)^2 dgamma) log(integral f(x)^2 dgamma) <= 2 integral ||gradient f(x)||^2 dgamma.\nThe gradient is the Riesz representation of the real Frechet derivative and the norm is the Hilbert norm. All integrals are real Bochner integrals; log is totalized at zero and the product t log t is interpreted continuously at t=0. The assertion requires no positivity of f or of its square mass, and no normalization, compact support, additional cutoff limit, or inequality certificate.",
  "reconstructed_text_sha256": "281c97502ff8201c939167953dae58ad987c5fa02e995adfc9c5f814f1cf493a",
  "objects": [
    "A real inner-product space E with compatible norm and Borel measurable structure.",
    "The canonical centered variance-one Gaussian measure gamma on E.",
    "A signed function f : E -> real numbers and its global gradient.",
    "The squared-function entropy and the squared Hilbert norm of the gradient."
  ],
  "domains": [
    "E is finite dimensional and complete; dimension zero is included.",
    "f is defined on all of E and takes arbitrary real values.",
    "Integration is over all E with respect to gamma; f(x)^2 and its integral are nonnegative, possibly zero."
  ],
  "quantifiers": [
    "Universally quantified over every E satisfying the listed structural instances.",
    "Universally quantified over every f satisfying the smoothness and integrability hypotheses.",
    "gamma is fixed by E through the canonical standard Gaussian construction, not an extra universally variable measure."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "E is a normed additive commutative group and a real inner-product space, complete and finite dimensional, with its Borel measurable structure.",
    "f is globally twice continuously Frechet differentiable.",
    "f is AE strongly measurable and has finite L2 seminorm under gamma.",
    "gradient f is AE strongly measurable and has finite L2 seminorm under gamma.",
    "x -> f(x)^2 log(f(x)^2) is AE strongly measurable and has finite integral of its absolute value under gamma."
  ],
  "conclusion": [
    "Writing M = integral f(x)^2 dgamma, integral f(x)^2 log(f(x)^2) dgamma - M log(M) <= 2 integral ||gradient f(x)||^2 dgamma."
  ],
  "scopes_and_senses": [
    "gamma is the canonical pushforward of the independent real standard Gaussian product through a canonical orthonormal-basis map.",
    "gradient f is the literal global Riesz inverse applied to the real Frechet derivative, not an arbitrary representative.",
    "The norm is the compatible Hilbert norm.",
    "The integrals are real Bochner integrals with genuine integrability hypotheses.",
    "Real.log is totalized at zero; t log(t) has value zero at t=0 in this use.",
    "The subtraction is homogeneous square entropy, with no unit-mass normalization.",
    "No f positivity, positive square mass, compact support, cutoff convergence, or desired-inequality premise occurs."
  ],
  "constant_dependencies": [
    "The displayed coefficient is exactly 2, uniformly independent of E, its dimension, f, and the square mass M.",
    "The Gaussian normalization is centered with variance one in each orthonormal coordinate.",
